// The one field-less type that is NOT shared: one that declares a
// destructor. An immortal object is never released, so its `drop` would
// never run -- silently, at every construction -- and that IS observable.
// So a field-less type with a destructor keeps allocating, one object per
// construction, and its destructor runs once per object exactly as before.
//
// The alternative was to refuse the combination. It was rejected: a
// field-less type with a destructor is a legitimate guard whose whole
// content is its effect, and an allocation optimisation is not a reason to
// take a working shape out of the language.
type Tick {}
void Tick.drop() { print("tick"); }

void three() {
  Tick a = Tick();
  Tick b = Tick();
  Tick c = Tick();
  print("made 3");
}

three();

// One at a time, so the print order shows each object dying on its own
// rather than three at the end of a scope.
int i = 0;
while (i < 2) {
  Tick t = Tick();
  print("loop");
  i = i + 1;
}
print("done");
